_{3}\\)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal), the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical), or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nScanning the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) gives the following candidates:\n\n| Direction | Equation | Points of \\(T_{3}\\) on it |\n|-----------|----------|---------------------------|\n| Horizontal| \\(y=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| Horizontal| \\(y=2\\)  | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| Horizontal| \\(y=3\\)  | \\((1,3)\\) |\n| Vertical  | \\(x=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| Vertical  | \\(x=2\\)  | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| Vertical  | \\(x=3\\)  | \\((3,1)\\) |\n| Slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) |\n| Slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| Slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n\nNo other line parallel to one of the forbidden directions contains a point of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Elimination of each candidate\n\nFor each candidate \\(L\\) we list the points of \\(T_{3}\\) **not** on \\(L\\) and examine whether they can be covered by two sunny lines.  \nRecall that a sunny line may have any slope except \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\).  \nThus two points can share a sunny line **iff** the slope of the segment joining them is different from those three forbidden values.\n\nBelow we give, for each \\(L\\), the set \\(R\\) of remaining points and the slopes of all unordered pairs in \\(R\\).\n\n| \\(L\\) | Remaining set \\(R\\) | Slopes of pairs in \\(R\\) (forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) |\n|------|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) | \\((1,2)-(1,3):\\infty\\); \\((1,2)-(2,2):0\\); \\((1,3)-(2,2):-1\\) |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\) | \\((1,1)-(2,1):0\\); \\((1,1)-(3,1):0\\); \\((2,1)-(3,1):0\\); \\((1,1)-(1,3):\\infty\\); \\((1,3)-(2,1):-2\\) (allowed) ; \\((1,3)-(3,1):-