=3\\)**  \n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nDefine the three lines\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n| Point | Belongs to which line? |\n|-------|------------------------|\n| (1,1) | \\(L_1\\) because \\(1 = 1\\) |\n| (2,2) | \\(L_1\\) because \\(2 = 2\\) |\n| (1,2) | \\(L_2\\) because \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac32\\) |\n| (3,1) | \\(L_2\\) because \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac32\\) |\n| (1,3) | \\(L_3\\) because \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5\\) |\n| (2,1) | \\(L_3\\) because \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5\\) |\n\nAll six points are covered.\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nThe slopes of \\(L_1, L_2, L_3\\) are respectively \\(1,\\ -\\tfrac12,\\ -2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each line is sunny, and the three lines are distinct.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly three sunny lines exists, i.e. \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\nBy taking any subset of the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) together with the three sunny lines above we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\). Hence for \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\text{ are all attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**4.  Remarks on the general case**\n\nThe two constructions above settle the extremal value \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\) and show that the maximal value \\(k=n\\) is attainable at least for \\(n=3\\).  \nThe problem of determining, for an arbitrary \\(n\\ge 3\\), which integers \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) admit a configuration remains open. A complete answer would require either:\n\n* a general method producing, for any prescribed \\(k\\), a set of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering \\(T_n\\); or  \n* a proof that certain values of \\(k\\) cannot be realized (for example by counting arguments or parity considerations).\n\nAt present such a general construction or impossibility proof has not been found."
