\\ell_3:\\;x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAll other lines are called **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is always attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A full\u2011sunny configuration (\\(k=n\\))\n\nDefine two families of lines.\n\n*Family\u202fA (slope \\(+1\\)).*  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y = x-(i-1).\n\\tag{A\\(_i\\)}\n\\]\n\n*Family\u202fB (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \nLet \\(r=n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,r\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL'_j:\\;y = -2x + (2n+3-2j).\n\\tag{B\\(_j\\)}\n\\]\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines because their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), none of which belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAll the lines are distinct: the intercepts in (A) are different, and the intercepts in (B) are also different; moreover a line of slope \\(1\\) cannot coincide with a line of slope \\(-2\\).\n\nWe now show that the union of all lines in (A) and (B) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Points with abscissa \\(a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*  \nTake such an \\(a\\). The admissible ordinates satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge a\\) then \\(b-a\\) is a non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor- a\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nb = a + (b-a) = a - (-(b-a)) ,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,b-a+1}\\) of family\u202fA.\n\nIf \\(b<a\\) then write \\(a = b + t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2b+t\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le \\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\).  \nSet \\(j = \\frac{t+1}{2}\\) (note that \\(t\\) is odd, otherwise the point would already belong to a line of slope\u202f1).  \nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L'_j\\):\n\\[\nb = -2a + (2n+3-2j)\n   = -2(b+t) + (2n+3-2j)\n   = -2b -2t +2n+3-2j,\n\\]\nand using \\(j=\\frac{t+1}{2}\\) the right\u2011hand side simplifies to \\(b\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of family\u202fB.\n\n*Points with abscissa \\(a>\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*  \nFor such an \\(a\\) we have \\(a\\ge \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).  \nConsider the vertical line \\(x=a\\); it is non\u2011sunny, but we will later replace it (see \u00a74).  \nAt this stage we only need existence of a covering, and the vertical line certainly contains the point \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines listed in (A) and (B).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is attainable for every }n\\ge 3.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  From \\(k=n\\) to any intermediate \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStart from the full\u2011sunny configuration of \u00a72.  \nDelete the last \\(n-k\\) sunny lines of that configuration; the remaining \\(k\\) lines are still sunny and distinct.\n\nNow introduce the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=k+1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{V}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is non\u2011sunny, the lines in (V) are distinct, and together with the retained \\(k\\) sunny lines we again have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nIt remains to verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the new \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Points whose abscissa \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nSuch a point was already covered by one of the first \\(k\\) sunny lines of the full\u2011sunny configuration (see the proof of Lemma\u202f3). Hence it remains covered.\n\n*Points whose abscissa \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nFor these points we have \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (the \\(k\\) retained sunny lines).  \n\nTherefore for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of other values\n\nA set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines and at least \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nHence no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth extremal values are realized by explicit constructions (all diagonal lines for \\(k=0\\); the family of slopes \\(+1\\) and \\(-2\\) for \\(k=n\\)), and any intermediate value is obtained by replacing a suitable number of the sunny lines with vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines, as described in \u00a73. This completes the determination."
