            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;y=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_{y}:\\;x=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a **non‑sunny** line must be of one of the three types  \n\n\\[\n\\text{vertical }x=c,\\qquad\n\\text{horizontal }y=c,\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nWe examine each possible value of \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (all three lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny.  Verification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point}&\\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)&L_{1},L_{2}\\\\\n(1,2)&L_{1}\\\\\n(1,3)&L_{1},L_{3}\\\\\n(2,1)&L_{2}\\\\\n(2,2)&L_{3}\\\\\n(3,1)&L_{2},L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and exactly \\(0\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (one sunny line, two non‑sunny lines)\n\n**Construction A.**  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nL_{2}:x+y=4\\quad(\\text{diagonal}),\\qquad\nL_{3}:y=x\\quad(\\text{sunny}).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\) covers all points of \\(T_{3}\\) except \\((2,1)\\).  \nSince \\((2,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), the sunny line \\(L_{3}\\) covers the missing point.  \nAll three lines are distinct, and exactly one of them ( \\(L_{3}\\) ) is sunny.\n\n**Construction B (alternative).**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:y=x\\;(\\text{sunny}).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\) leaves only \\((2,2)\\) uncovered; \\((2,2)\\) lies on \\(y=x\\).  \nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\) (two sunny lines, one non‑sunny line)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \n*Proof.*  Any two distinct points of \\(T_{3}\\) determine a line whose slope is one of  \n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1,1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}\\).  \nThe slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) correspond to the three forbidden directions; the remaining slopes are admissible.  \nNo three points of \\(T_{3}\\) share any admissible slope, so a sunny line cannot pass through three of them. ∎\n\nConsequently two sunny lines together cover at most four points, leaving at least two points to be covered by the single non‑sunny line.\n\nA non‑sunny line must be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \nWe examine each possibility.\n\n*Vertical non‑sunny line.*  \nIf the vertical line is \\(x=1\\), it covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); the remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nAny pair among these three has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), all forbidden for sunny lines. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the three remaining points.\n\nThe same obstruction occurs for \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=3\\) (a quick check of the remaining points shows that every pair among them has a forbidden slope).  \n\n*Horizontal non‑sunny line.*  \nIf the horizontal line is \\(y=1\\), it covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); the leftovers \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) again contain only forbidden slopes between any two of them.  \nThe cases \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=3\\) are analogous and also impossible.\n\n*Diagonal non‑sunny line.*  \nThe three possible diagonals are \\(x+y=2,\\;3,\\;4\\).  \nFor \\(x+y=2\\) only \\((1,1)\\) is covered, leaving five points—far more than two sunny lines can handle.  \nFor \\(x+y=3\\) the uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nAmong these, the only admissible pair is \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (slope 1); the other two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) cannot be joined by a sunny line.  \nFor \\(x+y=4\\) the uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\); any two of them have a forbidden slope.  \n\nThus **no** choice of a single non‑sunny line allows the two remaining sunny lines to cover all points.  \nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\nSince each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\), we need a partition of the six points into three admissible pairs.  \nThe admissible pairs (i.e. pairs whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)) are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&:\\;(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nP_{2}&:\\;(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nP_{3}&:\\;(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &&\\text{slope }-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese three pairs are disjoint and together contain all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nThe corresponding sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;x+2y=5\\quad(\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;2x+y=5\\quad(\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nEach point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on exactly one of these lines, so the three sunny lines cover the whole set.\n\n--