e \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k\\) as in (1) and put \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).\n   Choose the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the\n   chosen vertical lines are pairwise parallel, therefore they do not\n   intersect inside \\(T_n\\) and the points they cover are disjoint.\n   Consequently they cover\n\n   \\[\n   C_m=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The number of points that remain uncovered is\n\n   \\[\n   R_m=|T_n|-C_m\n        =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl[m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\Bigr]\n        =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m=n-k\\) and \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\), we have\n   \\(n-m=k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\); hence \\(R_m\\le 2k\\).  Pair the remaining\n   points arbitrarily (if an odd number remains, leave one point single);\n   each pair determines a unique line whose slope is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), i.e. a sunny line.  Using at most \\(k\\) sunny lines we\n   cover all points left after the vertical lines.\n\n   The construction therefore yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the bound (1) gives \\(k\\le1\\); the constructions for\n   \\(k=0\\) (three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\)) and for\n   \\(k=1\\) (vertical \\(x=1\\), horizontal \\(y=1\\) and the sunny line\n   \\(y=x\\)) realise both possibilities.\n\n   For \\(n=4\\) the bound gives \\(k\\le1\\), but a direct construction shows\n   that \\(k=3\\) is also possible:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{non‑sunny line }&x+y=5,\\\\\n   &\\text{sunny lines }&\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\;\n                         \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac52,\\;\n                         \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   (Each of the four required points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le5\\) lies on at\n   least one of these lines.)  Hence for \\(n=4\\) the set of admissible\n   \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\); this does not contradict the general bound,\n   because the bound is *sharp* for all \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V:\\;x=c,\\qquad \\mathcal H:\\;y=c,\\qquad \\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwith a constant \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),\n(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\), then the slope would be\n\\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}\\).\nBecause all coordinates are integers, the slope would be a rational\nnumber with denominator dividing the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates.\nIf the slope were \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel\nto one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of\n“sunny”.  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a line of type \\(\\mathcal V\\) we have \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\);\nthe admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most\n\\(n\\) values.  The same bound holds for the families \\(\\mathcal H\\) and\n\\(\\mathcal D\\). ∎\n\n\n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines.  Counting points and using Lemmas 1 and 2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;k\\cdot 2+(n-k)\\cdot n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible integer \\(k\\) must satisfy\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (7)\n\nFix a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) with (7) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the lines\nare parallel, hence they do not intersect inside \\(T_n\\).  Consequently\nthe set of points covered by the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) has cardinality\n\n\\[\nC_m=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 – points that remain uncovered.**  \nFrom (4) and (9) the number of uncovered points equals\n\n\\[\nR_m=|T_n|-C_m\n    =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl[m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\Bigr]\n    =\\frac{(n-m)(n-m-1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m=n-k\\) and \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\), we have\n\\(n-m=k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nR_m\\le 2k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3 – sunny lines.**  \nList the \\(R_m\\) uncovered points.  Pair them arbitrarily; if \\(R_m\\) is\nodd, leave one point unpaired.  Every pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset T_n\\) with\n\\(P\\neq Q\\) determines a unique line whose slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise the two points would lie on a non‑sunny line,\nwhich is impossible because all non‑sunny lines have already been\nused).  Hence each pair is covered by a sunny line.  The at most \\(k\\)\nsunny lines obtained in this way cover all remaining points.\n\nAll together we have \\(m\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and at most \\(k\\)\nsunny lines; by (11) the number of sunny lines needed does not exceed\n\\(k\\).  If fewer than \\(k\\) sunny lines are required we may add any\nadditional sunny lines (for instance, lines of slope \\(1\\) passing through\na point already covered) so that the total number of sunny lines is\nexactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) there exists a set of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that covers every point of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  Formula (7) gives \\(k\\le1\\).  \n  – \\(k=0\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\).  \n  – \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\).\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  Formula (7) yields \\(k\\le1\\), but a direct construction shows\n  that \\(k=3\\) is also possible (see the construction in the summary).\n  Hence for \\(n=4\\) the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the bound (7) is sharp; the construction of\n  Section 2 produces configurations for all \\(k\\) with\n  \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\), and Lemma 1 proves that no larger\n  \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nwith the sole exceptional case \\(n=4\\), where the additional value\n\\(k=3\\) is also attainable.  The upper bound follows from a simple\ncounting argument, and the constructions given above show that every\nvalue within the bound can indeed be realized. ∎"
