\]\n\n   All \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(+1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\). Set \\(d=b-a\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   -(a-1)\\le d\\le k+1-2a .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side of (4) is an integer between \\(-(k-1)\\) and \\(k-1\\) and has the same parity as \\(k+1\\). Consequently there exists a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   d = k+1-2i .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (5) is precisely the condition that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \n   Distinctness of the \\(\\ell_i\\) follows from the distinct intercepts \\(k+1-2i\\). ∎\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Because the chosen vertical lines are parallel, the sets of points they cover are disjoint. Hence they cover  \n\n   \\[\n   C_m=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the vertical lines are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\(a' = a-m\\) (so that \\(a'\\ge1\\)) the condition\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  Thus the uncovered set is\n   exactly \\(T_k\\) (see (2)) and has cardinality \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines.*  \n   By the Lemma of §3, the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) given by (3).  These lines are distinct,\n   sunny, and none of them is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – total family.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines (6) together with\n   the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) contains exactly \\(n\\)\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every\n   point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required\n   configuration exists.\n\n5. **Small values check.**  \n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The construction with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) yields respectively  \n   – three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\);  \n   – two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) plus the sunny line \\(y=x\\);  \n   – one vertical line \\(x=1\\) plus the two sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and\n     \\(y=2x\\);  \n   – three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;y=\\tfrac12x\\).  \n   All satisfy the conditions.  \n   *\\(n=4,5,\\dots\\)* are handled by the general construction above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V:\\;x=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal H:\\;y=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (covering a triangular lattice by sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\ge1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nis the disjoint union of the \\(k\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y = x + (k+1-2i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(+1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).  \nFrom \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-(a-1)\\le d\\le k+1-2a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (5) runs through all integers of the same parity as\n\\(k+1\\) between \\(-(k-1)\\) and \\(k-1\\). Consequently there exists a unique\n\\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nd = k+1-2i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquation (6) is exactly the condition that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nBecause the intercepts \\(k+1-2i\\) are distinct, the lines are distinct.\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nFix integers \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny (vertical) lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the\n\\(L_i\\) are pairwise parallel, hence the sets of points they cover are\ndisjoint.  Consequently the total number of points covered by the family\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nC_m=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)\n     =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  The uncovered points  \n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by the vertical lines are those with\n\\(a\\ge m+1\\).  Writing \\(a'=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)) the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  Hence the uncovered set is\nexactly \\(T_k\\) defined in (3); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Sunny lines covering the uncovered set  \n\nBy Lemma 1, the set \\(T_k\\) is the disjoint union of the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) given by (4).  These lines are distinct,\nsunny, and none of them belongs to the families (2).\n\n#### 4.  The final family  \n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and it covers every