er the\n> triangular set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible  \n\nLet \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nConsider an arbitrary sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel\nto any line of the family \\(\\mathcal D\\), it meets each diagonal\n\\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  The diagonals intersecting \\(T_k\\) are\n\\(c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); there are \\(k\\) of them.  Moreover, a sunny line\ncannot meet two *consecutive* diagonals of \\(T_k\\) simultaneously, for the\nsegment joining a point of diagonal \\(c\\) to a point of diagonal \\(c+1\\) has\nslope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets at most every second\ndiagonal, i.e. at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k}{2}=t\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nSince \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2=\\dfrac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)\\), covering all\npoints of \\(T_k\\) needs at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|T_k|}{t}=2t+1=k+1\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Therefore a covering with only \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible\nwhen \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:\\;y=x\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(+1\\); it is sunny.  For every integer \\(j\\) with\n\\(1\\le j\\le t+1\\) we have \\(j+j=2j\\le 2t+2=k+1\\), hence the points  \n\n\\[\n(j,j)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,t+1)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_k\\) and lie on \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n*Pairs of points.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;t+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(t+1+i,\\;t+1-i).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(P_i,Q_i\\in T_k\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+(t+2-i)=t+2\\le 2t+2,\\qquad\n(t+1+i)+(t+1-i)=2t+2 .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Its slope is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(\\ell_i)=\\frac{t+1-i-(t+2-i)}{t+1+i-i}\n                         =\\frac{-1}{\\,t+1\\,},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each\n\\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  The intercepts of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct,\nhence the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_t\\) are pairwise different.\n\n*Covering.*  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\Delta\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le t\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(1+t=2t+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines.\nEvery point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\):\n* the points on the main diagonal are on \\(\\Delta\\);\n* any point with \\(a\\neq b\\) appears either as \\(P_i\\) or as \\(Q_i\\) for a\n  unique \\(i\\), and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus \\(T_k\\) is covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines whenever \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the original problem  \n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd (or \\(k=0,1\\)).*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nBy (6) the uncovered points are precisely \\(T_k\\); cover them with the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Section 3.  The total number of lines is\n\\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \nAny admissible configuration would contain a sub‑configuration covering\n\\(T_k\\); Section 2 shows that at least \\(k+1\\) sunny lines are required,\ncontradicting the requirement of having only \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence no\nsuch configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{\\,\\text{odd integers}\\,\\}\\ \\text{with }k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) or any odd integer not exceeding \\(n\\).  