ng with\n   exactly \\(k\\) lines would have to use **only** lines that attain the\n   maximum \\(t+1\\) points; otherwise the total would fall short.\n   By (5) there are only **two** distinct sunny lines that attain the\n   maximum, namely \\(L^{(1)}\\) and \\(L^{(2)}\\).  Since we need\n   \\(2t+1\\ge5\\) distinct lines, this is impossible.  Consequently no\n   configuration exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non‑sunny\n   and clearly cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and the\n   sunny line \\(y=x\\).  The vertical lines cover all points with\n   \\(a\\le n-1\\); the remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge n\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. they are exactly \\((n,1)\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) passes\n   through \\((n,1)\\) because \\(n=1\\) is impossible; instead we use the\n   sunny line \\(y=x+1\\), which contains the single uncovered point\n   \\((n,1)\\).  (Both lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(0\\) and\n   \\(1\\) respectively; the slope \\(1\\) line is sunny.)\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   As in the reduction, take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = -2x+5      &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y =  x         &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\),\n   and a direct substitution shows that each of the six points of \\(T_3\\) lies\n   on one of them.  Hence the required configuration exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the reduction of step 1 with the impossibility results of\n   steps 4 and 5 and the explicit constructions of step 6, we obtain that for\n   every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n**The final answer is \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}.**"
