l possible size.**  \n   For odd \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\) we have \\(|T_k|=(2t+1)(t+1)\\).  \n   From (1) the absolute maximum number of points a sunny line can contain is  \n\n   \\[\n   M(k)=\\max_{p,q} N_{p,q}(k)=t+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of (2).*  \n   If \\(q\\ge3\\) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+2}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le t-1\\), so \\(N_{p,q}(k)\\le t\\).  \n   Hence a line with \\(t+1\\) points must have \\(q=1\\) or \\(q=2\\).\n\n   *Case \\(q=1\\) (integer slope).*  \n   The line is \\(y=px+b\\) with integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   The condition \\(x+y\\le 2t+2\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (p+1)x+b\\le 2t+2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(p\\ge2\\) the left‑hand side grows at least \\(3x\\); (3) forces\n   \\(x\\le t\\), so at most \\(t\\) points occur.  \n   For \\(p\\le-3\\) the left‑hand side decreases at most by \\(2x\\); again\n   at most \\(t\\) admissible \\(x\\).  \n   The only possibilities giving \\(t+1\\) points are  \n\n   \\[\n   p=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad p=-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Case \\(q=2\\).*  \n   Then \\(p\\) is odd and the line has equation \\(y=\\frac{p}{2}x+b\\) with\n   \\(b\\in\\frac12\\mathbb Z\\).  Because \\(x\\) must be even, the number of\n   admissible abscissas is at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{2t+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil=t+1\\).\n   A direct check of the inequality \\(x+y\\le2t+2\\) shows that for every odd\n   \\(p\\) at most \\(t\\) points satisfy it.  Hence no line with \\(q=2\\) reaches\n   the bound \\(t+1\\).\n\n   Consequently the only sunny lines that contain the maximal number\n   \\(t+1\\) of points of \\(T_{2t+1}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L^{(1)} &: y = x \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)},\\\\\n   L^{(2)} &: y = -2x + (2t+3) \\qquad\\text{(slope }-2\\text{)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of them indeed contains the points  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(1)}:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t+1,t+1),\\qquad\n   L^{(2)}:\\;(1,2t+1