k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points; the line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{k+3}{2}\\) attains this bound.  \n   For \\(q\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side of (1) is \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\)\n\n   \\[\n   M(k):=\\max\\{\\text{# points of }T_k\\text{ on a sunny line}\\}\n          =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting argument.**  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) contains \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of them can cover at most \\(M(k)\\) points, so\n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot M(k)\\ge |T_k|\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (3) holds **iff** \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k\\) the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\dfrac{k^2}{2}<\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   Therefore **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can work**.\n\n5. **The odd case.**  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\).  \n   From (2) we have \\(M(k)=t+1\\).  Equality in (3) forces every sunny line to contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_{2t+1}\\).  \n   Using (1) with \\(q=1\\) or \\(q=2\\) one checks that the only sunny lines attaining \\(t+1\\) points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: y = -2x + (2t+3),\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2t+3}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (All three lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).)  \n   For each of them a direct substitution shows that the points\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: (1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t+1,t+1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: (1,2t+1),(2,2t-1),\\dots ,(t+1,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: (1,2t+2),(3,2t),\\dots ,(2t+1,2)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   belong to \\(T_{2t+1}\\); there are exactly \\(t+1\\) points on each line, and the three families are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n   Consequently, for a given \\(t\\) **at most three distinct sunny lines can achieve the required size**.  \n   Since we need \\(k=2t+1\\) distinct sunny lines, the only possibilities are  \n\n   * \\(t=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)) \u2013 we may use any one of the three lines;  \n   * \\(t=1\\) (\\(k=3\\)) \u2013 we can take the three lines listed in (4).  \n\n   For \\(t\\ge2\\) (\\(k\\ge5\\)) we would need more than three distinct sunny lines, which is impossible.  \n   Hence **the only admissible odd values are \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).**\n\n6. **Construction for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non\u2011sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   The only points not yet covered are those with \\(a=n\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\), i.e. the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  This line is distinct from the vertical ones and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Hence all required points are covered with exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The uncovered set is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -2x+5,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   **Point\u2011by\u2011point verification**\n\n   * \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) because \\(1=1\\) and \\(2=2\\).  \n   * \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) because \\(3=-2\\cdot1+5\\) and \\(1=-2\\cdot2+5\\).  \n   * \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) because \\(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52\\) and \\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52\\).\n\n   Every point of \\(T_3\\) appears on one of the three lines, so together with the vertical lines all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are covered.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the reduction of step\u202f1, the maximal\u2011size analysis of steps\u202f2\u20135, the impossibility for all \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,3\\}\\), and the explicit constructions of step\u202f6, we obtain that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]"
