 cover the translate of \\(T_k\\). The converse implication fails, so the reduction is not an equivalence.  \n\nBecause the later counting argument assumes that the sunny lines must *exactly* cover \\(T_k\\), the whole chain of reasoning built on this reduction collapses.  \n**Classification:** Critical Error.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Bounding the number of integer points on a sunny line\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cWrite a sunny line in reduced form \\(y=\\dfrac{p}{q}x+b\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) satisfying \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(q\\ge1\\) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n> For integer points on the line we must have \\(x\\equiv r\\pmod q\\) for a fixed residue \\(r\\).  \n> Moreover the inequality \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) gives  \n> \\[\n> x\\le\\frac{k+1-b}{1+\\frac{p}{q}} .\n> \\]  \n> Consequently the number of admissible integer abscissas is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe congruence condition \\(x\\equiv r\\pmod q\\) is correct *provided* the line contains at least one integer point (which forces \\(b\\) to be rational with denominator dividing \\(q\\)).  \n\nThe inequality \\(x\\le\\frac{k+1-b}{1+\\frac{p}{q}}\\) is derived from  \n\\(x+y = x + \\frac{p}{q}x + b \\le k+1\\).  \nIf \\(1+\\frac{p}{q}>0\\) (i.e. slope \\(p/q>-1\\)), the inequality indeed yields an **upper** bound on \\(x\\).  \nIf \\(1+\\frac{p}{q}<0\\) (i.e. slope \\(p/q<-1\\)), the inequality sign must be **reversed**, giving a **lower** bound on \\(x\\). The solution ignores this case and proceeds as if an upper bound always holds.\n\nBecause the sign error is not addressed, the derivation of (1) is mathematically incorrect for slopes less than \\(-1\\). The statement (1) itself happens to be true (any arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\) inside the interval \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) contains at most \\(\\lfloor k/q\\rfloor+1\\) terms), but the proof given is invalid.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (faulty reasoning).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Maximal size of a sunny line in \\(T_k\\)\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cFrom (1) the largest possible cardinality of a sunny line is obtained for the smallest denominator \\(q\\).  \n> *If \\(q=1\\) (integer slope) we obtain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points (the line \\(y=x\\) shows that this bound is attained).  \n> *If \\(q=2\\) (half\u2011integer slope) we obtain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points; the line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{k+3}{2}\\) attains this bound.  \n> For \\(q\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side of (1) is \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n> Hence for every \\(k\\)  \n> \\[\n> M(k):=\\max\\{\\#\\text{points of }T_k\\text{ on a sunny line}\\}\n> =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \n\n- The bound for \\(q=1\\) (integer slope) is correct: a line of slope \\(1\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points, and the line \\(y=x\\) achieves it.  \n\n- The claim for \\(q=2\\) is **false**.  \n  For many values of \\(k\\) (e.g. \\(k=4,5\\)) the line \\(y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{k+3}{2}\\) contains **fewer** than \\(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor+1\\) integer points (often none). A direct check shows the bound is not attained. Hence the statement \u201cthe line \u2026 attains this bound\u201d is a factual error.  \n\n- The argument for \\(q\\ge3\\) again relies on the (incorrect) inequality (1) derived in Step\u202f2. While the final inequality \\(\\lfloor k/3\\rfloor+1\\le\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) is true, the justification that the bound \\(\\lfloor k/q\\rfloor+1\\) holds for all \\(q\\ge3\\) is based on the flawed derivation.  \n\nBecause the maximal size \\(M(k)\\) is a cornerstone of the later counting argument, the incorrect claim about the half\u2011integer case and the reliance on the faulty bound make the determination \\(M(k)=\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) **unjustified**.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (false claim about attainment) and also a Justification Gap (the derivation of the bound for \\(q\\ge3\\) is not rigorous).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 Counting argument\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cThe set \\(T_k\\) contains \\(|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n> If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of them can cover at most \\(M(k)\\) points, so  \n> \\[\n> k\\cdot M(k)\\ge |T_k|\n> \\Longrightarrow\n> k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Equality in (3) holds **iff** \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k\\) the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}{2}<\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n> Therefore **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can work**.\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe inequality (3) follows directly from the definition of \\(M(k)\\). However, the value of \\(M(k)\\) was derived on the basis of the flawed arguments in Steps\u202f2\u20133. Consequently, the conclusion that even \\(k\\) are impossible rests on an unsound bound.  \n\nEven if we temporarily accept the (incorrect) bound \\(M(k)=\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\), the parity argument would be valid *under that assumption*. Since the assumption is false, the conclusion is not justified.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (the conclusion depends on an invalid bound).\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f5 \u2013 The odd case and the claim that only \\(k=1,3\\) work\n\n> **Quote:** \u201cWrite \\(k=2t+1\\).  \n> From (2) we have \\(M(k)=t+1\\). Equality in (3) forces every sunny line to contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_{2t+1}\\).  \n> Using (1) with \\(q=1\\) or \\(q=2\\) one checks that the only sunny lines attaining \\(t+1\\) points are  \n> \\[\n> L_1:y=x,\\; L_2:y=-2x+(2t+3),\\; L_3:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2t+3}{2}.\n> \\]  \n> Hence at most three distinct sunny lines can achieve the required size.  \n> Therefore the only admissible odd values are \\(k=1\\) (any one of the three lines) and \\(k=3\\) (the three lines above).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe identification of the three families of lines is plausible, but it relies on the previously unproved claim that *only* those lines can achieve the maximal number of points. The argument \u201cusing (1) with \\(q=1\\) or \\(q=2\\) one checks \u2026\u201d is a **justification gap**: no detailed verification is provided that no other slope (e.g., \\(p/q=3\\) or \\(p/q=-3\\)) can also yield \\(t+1\\) points.  \n\nEven granting this claim, the conclusion that \u201cat most three distinct sunny lines can achieve the required size\u201d does **not** preclude the possibility of using more than three sunny lines that each contain fewer than the maximal number of points but together still cover all points of \\(T_{2t+1}\\). The counting argument in Step\u202f4 already forced each line to contain exactly the maximal number of points, but that forced equality depends on the (incorrect) bound for \\(M(k)\\).  \n\nThus the deduction that only \\(k=1,3\\) are possible is not rigorously established.  \n\n**Classification:** Justification Gap (insufficient justification for the uniqueness of the three lines) and, because it builds on earlier critical errors, its conclusion is unreliable.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n> **Quote:**  \n> *\\(k=0\\):* take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n> *\\(k=1\\):* take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (e.g. \\(y=2x-2n+1\\)).  \n> *\\(k=3\\):* take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\); a point\u2011by\u2011point check shows they cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are correct.  \nThe construction for \\(k=3\\) correctly covers the set \\(T_3\\) and therefore works for any \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nThus the **construction part** of the solution is sound; however, because the preceding impossibility arguments are flawed, the claim that these are the *only* admissible values is false.\n\n**Classification:** No error in the constructions themselves.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution\u2019s core reduction (Step\u202f1) is invalid, the bound on the number of integer points per sunny line (Step\u202f2) is derived incorrectly, and the claim that a specific half\u2011integer line attains the bound (Step\u202f3) is false. These critical errors invalidate the counting argument (Step\u202f4) and the parity conclusion (Step\u202f5). Consequently the final answer \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is **incorrect**; a simple counter\u2011example shows that \\(k=2\\) is feasible for \\(n=3\\) (e.g. the three lines \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;x=2\\)).  \n\nTherefore the solution does **not** solve the problem correctly."

