l\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, if \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(|q|\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the line would be\n   horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)), and the condition \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) forces\n   at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor k/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points.  Hence for **every** sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;|\\,\\text{line}\\cap T_k\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   and the bound is attained (see \u00a75).\n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   The triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge |T_k|\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2m\\).  Then the left\u2013hand side of (8) equals\n   \\(2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas the right\u2013hand side equals\n   \\(m(2m+1)=k^{2}/2+m\\).  Because \\(m>0\\), (8) is false.  \n   Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) works**.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2m+1\\).  Then\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (8) becomes an equality:\n\n   \\[\n   (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently every sunny line must contain **exactly**\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) points of \\(T_k\\), and the\n   \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.\n\n4. **Sunny lines attaining the bound.**  \n   Solving (2) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three families of lines that contain\n   \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&: y = x ,\\\\\n   L_{2}&: y = -2x + (2m+3),\\\\\n   L_{3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2m+3}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution shows\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&\\cap T_{2m+1}= \\{(i,i)\\mid